From 2315fd39fd8051029848575f98d242ff161ea3e3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 15:17:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] fix(preview): answer preview hostnames honestly while
 their sandbox is down
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two gaps left over from the previous two commits, both of which show a user the
wrong thing without erroring.

**The marketing homepage in the preview pane.** Preview hostnames are wildcard
DNS and only have a backend while that project's container is alive. Traefik
routes by Host, so from the moment a boot tears the old container down until the
new one registers, nothing claims the hostname and the request falls through to
this app — which cheerfully renders the LifemarkAI homepage. Inside a preview
pane that reads as "your app has been replaced by an ad", and a preview link
shared with a colleague shows them a signup page instead of the thing they were
sent to look at.

The app now recognises those hostnames and answers with a plain "this preview is
starting" page (503, self-refreshing with backoff) rather than pretending to be
the product. The editor no longer frames a preview URL until a probe confirms
it, so the normal flow never lands here — this is for the paths that bypass the
editor's state machine: a shared link, a manual reload mid-boot, a bookmark to a
project whose sandbox has since been reclaimed.

isSandboxPreviewHost is deliberately narrow and tested, because the failure mode
if it over-matches is the whole product serving a spinner: one label in front of
the configured domain, never the bare domain, never the app's own host even when
that host lives under the preview domain, and no match on a lookalike that
merely ends with the same characters. The check runs through a server fn so the
host helpers stay out of the client bundle — verified absent from dist/client.

**The fallback bridge never learned the handshake.** The previous commit taught
the sandbox bridge to answer lifemark-preview-ping, which is what makes escape
detection mean something. The srcdoc fallback engine speaks the same message
contract but was left behind, so a fallback preview that had reported a location
once would be read as an escaped frame on its next load and reset to "/". It now
answers pings and sends origin/href, same as the sandbox bridge.

11 new tests pass. tsc --noEmit: 51 errors before, 51 after, identical per file.
Production build passes.
